A dry run on a triangle with two colors colors the first two vertices differently. The third vertex touches both colors, so both choices fail and the method returns false.

The worst-case time is `O(m^n * n^2)` because choosing a vertex and counting its neighbor colors can scan the whole matrix. The color array and recursion use `O(n + m)` extra space.

Here is an iterative version of the search. It stores the next color to try for every vertex, so there is no recursive call stack. When a vertex cannot use any remaining color, it moves back and continues from the previous vertex.

The color values are kept as zero-based integers. For an empty graph, the answer is true. For a non-empty graph with no available colors, it is false.

```go
func GraphColoring(edges [][]int, m, n int) bool {
	if n == 0 {
		return true
	}
	if m <= 0 {
		return false
	}

	colors := make([]int, n)
	nextColor := make([]int, n)
	for i := range colors {
		colors[i] = -1
	}

	vertex := 0
	for vertex >= 0 && vertex < n {
		placed := false

		for color := nextColor[vertex]; color < m; color++ {
			valid := true
			for neighbor := 0; neighbor < n; neighbor++ {
				if edges[vertex][neighbor] != 0 && colors[neighbor] == color {
					valid = false
					break
				}
			}

			if valid {
				colors[vertex] = color
				nextColor[vertex] = color + 1
				placed = true
				break
			}
		}

		if placed {
			vertex++
			if vertex < n {
				nextColor[vertex] = 0
			}
		} else {
			nextColor[vertex] = 0
			colors[vertex] = -1
			vertex--
			if vertex >= 0 {
				colors[vertex] = -1
			}
		}
	}

	return vertex == n
}
```

There can be `m^n` color combinations, and checking one vertex scans `n` neighbors, so the worst-case time is `O(m^n * n)`. The arrays use `O(n)` extra space. This version avoids recursion, but it still has the same backtracking search.

